<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/paths/level5-binomial-opening> {
  ex:path-level5-binomial-opening cd:forTopic ex:poisson-distribution ; cd:hasStep ex:l5-p07a-step, ex:l5-p07b-step, ex:l5-p07c-step, ex:l5-p07d-step .
  ex:l5-p07a-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 8 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-p07a-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-p07a-title, ex:l5-p07a-context, ex:l5-p07a-media ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07b-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 9 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-p07b-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-p07b-title, ex:l5-poisson-chart, ex:l5-poisson-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07c-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 10 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-p07c-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-p07c-title, ex:l5-volume-chart, ex:l5-volume-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07d-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 11 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-p07d-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-p07d-title, ex:l5-p07d-context, ex:l5-poisson-limit-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-poisson> {
  ex:l5-p07a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:poisson-distribution ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-p07a-heading, ex:l5-p07a-text, ex:l5-p07a-image ; cd:narration "The investigation now concerns detected particle intensity rather than positive-finding frequency. We analyse a selected 100 mL portion rather than the whole water body. In an actual investigation, a detected count relative to examined volume informs that intensity; selection of portions, recovery and detection determine its environmental scope. The filter illustrations show 2, 4 and 6 particles, constructed examples, not measurements or a simulated sample used to estimate a parameter. Their variation motivates a counting model, not an inference that the true intensity has been measured. An ideal homogeneous independent event process gives Poisson counts. Spatial clustering, heterogeneous concentrations, preparation losses and dependent detection can violate it. Greater observed variance than mean motivates checking those mechanisms; a negative-binomial model is an alternative, not an automatic conclusion. Next use specified intensity four detected particles per 100 mL to explain the count variability an intensity estimate must accommodate. This detected intensity need not equal the true particle concentration, and a larger volume alone does not validate representative selection."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:poisson-distribution ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-p07b-heading, ex:l5-p07b-chart, ex:l5-p07b-math ; cd:narration "P07b. This is an exact model probability chart, not the frequencies of the three illustrations. The expected detected count is four particles per 100 mL. Each ordinary bar gives the probability of one integer count; 17+ collects every count from seventeen upwards. No mass has been discarded or renormalized and seventeen is not an upper bound. Zero detected particles has probability 0.01831563889, about 1.83%; at least one has probability about 98.17%. These statements assume the model and parameter, not their truth. Expected count and variance are numerically equal, with counts and squared counts respectively. Equality is an ideal Poisson property, not a universal property of particle observations. A zero detection does not establish absence. The authored formula and complete symbol legend are visible together from the start."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:poisson-distribution ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-p07c-heading, ex:l5-p07c-chart, ex:l5-p07c-math ; cd:narration "P07c. The two distributions share one count axis and probability scale. Constant detected-event intensity of 0.04 particles per mL gives expected counts four in 100 mL and eight in 200 mL. The volume, not a Bernoulli trial count, is the exposure. The second distribution shifts towards larger counts and has larger absolute variance. This scaling requires homogeneous intensity and comparable preparation and detection performance across volumes; saturation or volume-dependent recovery can invalidate it. The final category contains all counts at least seventeen in each distribution. The model remains unbounded. Increasing volume raises the expected count, not a guaranteed measured count."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07d-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:poisson-distribution ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-p07d-heading, ex:l5-p07d-text, ex:l5-p07d-math ; cd:narration "P07d. Binomial has a fixed number of independent binary opportunities and a count bounded by that number. Poisson fixes an exposure and has no finite count bound. In the rare-event limit, the number of independent binomial trials grows, their common success probability shrinks and the expected count approaches a finite lambda. The binomial distribution then converges to Poisson. This is not an exact identity at finite trial counts or a rule that every small probability is well approximated for every tail event. At a fixed mean of four, binomial trial counts 20, 100 and 1000 have probabilities 0.2, 0.04 and 0.004; their zero-count probabilities approach the Poisson value 0.01831563889. Unequal Bernoulli probabilities instead give a Poisson-binomial count, a different model. This concludes the first Poisson explanation before the later distinction between mass, density and cumulative probability."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-p07a-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-p07a-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
